  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "A fractional index is another way of writing a root. The denominator of the index says which root to take and the numerator says which power to raise to, so $27^{\\frac{2}{3}}$ means \"cube root of $27$, then square it\". Because a fractional index is still an index, every index law you already use applies to it unchanged.",
    "facts": [
      "$a^{\\frac{1}{n}}=\\sqrt[n]{a}$ — the denominator of the index is the index of the root, so $64^{\\frac{1}{3}}=\\sqrt[3]{64}=4$.",
      "$a^{\\frac{m}{n}}=\\sqrt[n]{a^{m}}=\\left(\\sqrt[n]{a}\\right)^{m}$ — the denominator roots and the numerator powers.",
      "**Root first, then power.** $27^{\\frac{2}{3}}=\\left(\\sqrt[3]{27}\\right)^{2}=3^{2}=9$ keeps the numbers small; squaring first gives $729$ to cube-root.",
      "A negative fractional index still means a reciprocal: $a^{-\\frac{m}{n}}=\\frac{1}{a^{\\frac{m}{n}}}$, so $8^{-\\frac{2}{3}}=\\frac{1}{4}$.",
      "For a fraction base, root the numerator and the denominator separately: $\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{2}{3}$.",
      "The index laws hold for fractional indices — add when multiplying, subtract when dividing, multiply when raising a power to a power: $x^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\times x^{\\frac{1}{3}}=x^{\\frac{5}{6}}$.",
      "$a^{\\frac{1}{n}}$ has no real value when $a$ is negative and $n$ is even, because no real number raised to an even power gives a negative result."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Deal with a negative index first by writing the reciprocal.",
      "Take the root named by the denominator of the index.",
      "Raise that result to the power named by the numerator."
    ]
  },
